    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you go a bit deeper into the enzymatic part though? Like, what exactly happens at the binding site when the inhibitor meets the ACE enzyme to stop the conversion? I'm trying to visualize the actual mechanism of how the enzyme gets \"blocked.\"",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The binding mechanism of ACE inhibitors is a classic example of competitive inhibition, though it's often described as a pseudo-irreversible inhibition depending on the specific drug's structure. The ACE inhibitor molecule has a structure that mimics the natural substrate of the ACE enzyme, which is Angiotensin I. This mimicry allows the inhibitor to fit snugly into the active site of the enzyme, much like a perfectly shaped piece of mold, preventing Angiotensin I from docking correctly. Once bound, the inhibitor occupies the active site, effectively jamming the machinery so that the catalytic reaction—the conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II—cannot proceed. This blockage is what stops the cascade that raises blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
